#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228887 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "96494014-7289-548b-9749-2eb6336c9cef" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Solutions of Inhomogeneous BVPs"}] :question/id #uuid "837c1385-88d1-5909-b79a-83df25a66838" :question/math-academy-id "e-21798" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\ny'' + 16y = x^3 e^{x},  \\qquad y'(0) = y'(\\pi) = 0,\\quad 0\\leq x \\leq \\pi\n$$\n\nLet $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14077005/assets/b580aad5cc5933b519879a940442764e414a74e3ad3018e3b1f48191484a0f78.png)\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\quad$ The absolute value of $\\det(A)$ is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14077005/assets/9d59df7aed836f589b9744d0e1e594ef38e567fc17402deaad196a1b6768db09.png)\n\n$\\quad$ Therefore, the corresponding homogeneous BVP  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14077005/assets/471ff03c04cc9f360469d4c2f5f2e02ac091d211d7f6830b379997143dbd2ea1.png)\n\n$\\quad$ Therefore, our inhomogeneous BVP  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14077005/assets/ad34113aed97995e76ef24368fdce7b5c2b6cddc1678615e47b3ab61e04603bd.png)" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the second-order inhomogeneous boundary value problem (BVP)\n\n$$\ny''(x) + P(x)y' + Q(x)y = f(x), \\qquad a \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n\\alpha_1 y(a) + \\beta_1 y'(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad \n\\alpha_2 y(b) + \\beta_2 y'(b) = \\gamma_2\n$$\n\nwhere $P, Q,$ and $f$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and $\\alpha_i$ and $\\beta_i$ are real constants such that $(\\alpha_i, \\beta_i) \\neq (0,0).$ \n\nThe inhomogeneous BVP has at most one solution if and only if the associated homogeneous BVP has only the trivial solution $y \\equiv 0.$ Specifically:\n\n- If the homogeneous BVP has the unique solution $y \\equiv 0,$ then the inhomogeneous problem either has no solution or a unique solution.\n\n- If the homogeneous BVP has (infinitely many) nontrivial solutions, then the inhomogeneous problem either has infinitely many solutions or no solution.\n\nTo classify the solutions of the corresponding homogeneous BVP, we define the matrix $A$ as\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14077005/assets/f0ae121481e21c471d2e7b7eab072a2de438456dc76ac909db13b0262234fb80.png)\n\nThe homogeneous BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y \\equiv 0$ if and only if $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$ In other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the homogeneous BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Neumann boundary conditions. So, setting $\\alpha_i = 0, \\beta_i = 1, a = 0,$ and $b = \\pi,$ the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14077005/assets/f0cdf9d9e9f668853a863704ae8f72a6acd8842ffefff70a46e1d826f8b8a510.png)\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y = e^{m x}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+16 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m = \\pm 4\\textrm i.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\ny_1(x) = \\cos 4x, \\qquad y_2(x) = \\sin 4x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\ny_1'(x) = -4\\sin 4x, \\qquad y_2'(x) = 4\\cos 4x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot 0) & 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot 0) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\pi ) & 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\pi )\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(A)| & =|0\\cdot 4-4\\cdot 0| \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det(A) = 0,$ the corresponding homogeneous BVP $\\textrm{has infinitely many solutions}.$\n\nTherefore, since the corresponding homogeneous BVP has (infinitely many) nontrivial solutions, our inhomogeneous BVP $\\textrm{has infinitely many solutions or no solution}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383684 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "96494014-7289-548b-9749-2eb6336c9cef" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Solutions of Inhomogeneous BVPs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "237185f2-c459-5ea6-a1a7-41a0cc32ffcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383686} {:answer/id #uuid "4e441a55-8bb8-5f0b-aba9-de8e3d04a76e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383687} {:answer/id #uuid "6825c832-ea02-5e91-ba82-1ee6180e87d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383688} {:answer/id #uuid "cce30dd0-46b7-5f97-b588-3393a1a351b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383689} {:answer/id #uuid "221e5c9f-bb04-5c20-858c-53aa5306a8ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3}+1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cce30dd0-46b7-5f97-b588-3393a1a351b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383689} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca15f6e0-cf52-5437-b857-d3bc42080ee2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383685} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b173538-aa18-5071-a054-b5c9dc9748a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has the unique solution $y≡0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383692} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd75a95-2a4e-5047-8263-5ad136895f83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383693} {:answer/id #uuid "46eca456-5684-5f7e-a082-fee58c881c17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b173538-aa18-5071-a054-b5c9dc9748a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has the unique solution $y≡0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383692} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1b40453-f596-54a7-b120-5c9927ce0da6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383691} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48d798af-b879-50c0-9e84-ce7c6315b4f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383696} {:answer/id #uuid "e28a36fc-48c9-533a-a2ae-6b65ffa3f4f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383697} {:answer/id #uuid "75d2cf6a-e661-5667-80e1-2947297b3e0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has a unique solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383698} {:answer/id #uuid "41e8d75a-b03b-56ce-843f-9f2442f2d518" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has infinitely many solutions or no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75d2cf6a-e661-5667-80e1-2947297b3e0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has a unique solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383698} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb7cc1f9-8e34-536e-aca9-dedf41f35ce7" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8eb48225-775a-576c-bf42-f55c3f321d8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-338878" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-2y=4x,\\,y(0)=1,\\,y(1)=3,\\,0\\le x\\le 1\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ be fundamental solutions of the form $y={e}^{mx}$ to the corresponding homogeneous BVP, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(0) & {y}_{2}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(1) & {y}_{2}(1)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below **without calculating the solution** to this BVP.\n\n$\\,$ The **absolute value** of $det(A)$ is {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the corresponding homogeneous BVP {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, our inhomogeneous BVP {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the second-order inhomogeneous boundary value problem (BVP)\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $P,Q,$ and $f$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and ${\\alpha }_{i}$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real constants such that $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nThe inhomogeneous BVP has at most one solution if and only if the associated homogeneous BVP has only the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Specifically:\n\n- If the homogeneous BVP has the unique solution $y≡0,$ then the inhomogeneous problem has a unique solution.\n\n- If the homogeneous BVP has (infinitely many) nontrivial solutions, then the inhomogeneous problem either has infinitely many solutions or no solution.\n\nTo classify the solutions of the corresponding homogeneous BVP, we define the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\,{\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{1}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}(a) & {\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{2}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}(a) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{1}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}(b) & {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{2}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}(b)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe homogeneous BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y≡0$ if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$ In other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the homogeneous BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Dirichlet boundary conditions. So, setting ${\\alpha }_{i}=1,{\\beta }_{i}=0,a=0,$ and $b=1,$ the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(0) & {y}_{2}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(1) & {y}_{2}(1)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-m-2 & =0 \\\\ (m-2)(m+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=2$ and $m=-1,$ and so the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ {e}^{2} & {e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(A)| & =|{e}^{-1}-{e}^{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{e}-{e}^{2}| \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)\\ne 0,$ the corresponding homogeneous BVP $\\text{has the unique solution }y≡0.$\n\nTherefore, since the corresponding homogeneous BVP has only the trivial solution $y≡0$, our inhomogeneous BVP $\\text{has a unique solution}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383700 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "96494014-7289-548b-9749-2eb6336c9cef" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Solutions of Inhomogeneous BVPs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44171f40-d71b-5231-a251-c15424694a04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383702} {:answer/id #uuid "42354e5b-2350-5e6e-93a2-8a637d4c6db2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383703} {:answer/id #uuid "cc74053b-4bdd-51fb-a363-0e4ce161bdaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383704} {:answer/id #uuid "e948b823-e688-53ef-927c-7cf65d0c22a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383705} {:answer/id #uuid "15e4e182-11bc-570b-b689-6267e09c4b45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383706}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e948b823-e688-53ef-927c-7cf65d0c22a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383705} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4c57c1a-584b-5c8a-a811-7e0f2693a94f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383701} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "585fa1d5-d12a-58ab-92e6-fd3d521fb8f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has the unique solution $y≡0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383708} {:answer/id #uuid "0172266e-342e-5071-95ed-a38235de3c09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383709} {:answer/id #uuid "430d375b-252b-553f-bfae-89058dafb41d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "585fa1d5-d12a-58ab-92e6-fd3d521fb8f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has the unique solution $y≡0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383708} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6b6a3e6-5c96-50bd-876a-36a4bad9f667" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383707} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d25ffa10-39a0-5f5e-9a29-ccb89f7e8826" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383712} {:answer/id #uuid "7422c1c6-dda6-59db-8654-2442fbfed05d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has at most one solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383713} {:answer/id #uuid "8cd93abb-ef39-5752-9dfe-4969a2d7b66b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has a unique solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383714} {:answer/id #uuid "dd87b31d-5019-5abc-a1a1-64c575f179b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has infinitely many solutions or no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7422c1c6-dda6-59db-8654-2442fbfed05d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has at most one solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383713} :answer-field/id #uuid "82092c15-eeca-52c0-9056-be2c4158da3f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dfa31998-de06-5abd-90a7-06def4c1434e" :question/math-academy-id "q-339169" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y=x{e}^{3x},\\,{y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(0) & {y}_{2}^{′}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & {y}_{2}^{′}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below **without calculating the solution** to this BVP. \n\n$\\,$ The **absolute value** of $det(A)$ is {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the corresponding homogeneous BVP {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, our inhomogeneous BVP {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the second-order inhomogeneous boundary value problem (BVP)\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $P,Q,$ and $f$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and ${\\alpha }_{i}$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real constants such that $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nThe inhomogeneous BVP has at most one solution if and only if the associated homogeneous BVP has only the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Specifically:\n\n- If the homogeneous BVP has the unique solution $y≡0,$ then the inhomogeneous problem either has no solution or a unique solution.\n\n- If the homogeneous BVP has (infinitely many) nontrivial solutions, then the inhomogeneous problem either has infinitely many solutions or no solution.\n\nTo classify the solutions of the corresponding homogeneous BVP, we define the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\,{\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{1}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}(a) & {\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{2}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}(a) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{1}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}(b) & {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{2}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}(b)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe homogeneous BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y≡0$ if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$ In other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the homogeneous BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Neumann boundary conditions. So, setting ${\\alpha }_{i}=0,{\\beta }_{i}=1,a=0,$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(0) & {y}_{2}^{′}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & {y}_{2}^{′}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm \\text{i}.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}^{′}(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,{y}_{2}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\ -sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(A)| & =|0\\cdot 0-1\\cdot (-1)| \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)\\ne 0,$ the corresponding homogeneous BVP $\\text{has the unique solution }y≡0.$\n\nTherefore, since the corresponding homogeneous BVP has the unique solution $y≡0,$ our inhomogeneous BVP $\\text{has at most one solution}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383716 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "96494014-7289-548b-9749-2eb6336c9cef" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Solutions of Inhomogeneous BVPs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27a2e5a9-49ea-58b4-b669-07b6385c0346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383718} {:answer/id #uuid "c57a5e73-3c2e-51b2-b5c3-7934ec962306" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383719} {:answer/id #uuid "f7b4e1ab-5515-577d-bd32-b14bf34d74d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383720} {:answer/id #uuid "b7d1675f-b93e-5dce-b069-a3f6eed22b4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383721} {:answer/id #uuid "81afdbb9-1cad-5d80-a8a8-a5cea1490557" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383722}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7d1675f-b93e-5dce-b069-a3f6eed22b4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383721} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d018299-e8a5-5d0f-b061-5f1f4e6f46cf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383717} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c16b6758-c85a-52ff-9f21-0991b7822f93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has the unique solution $y≡0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383724} {:answer/id #uuid "1f2944e6-a82a-5494-9473-9243426558b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383725} {:answer/id #uuid "b4e5f568-17a1-5455-9498-e0f4a28c6932" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383726}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c16b6758-c85a-52ff-9f21-0991b7822f93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has the unique solution $y≡0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383724} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e8b707f-473c-539c-93e3-7d0e967eb97e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383723} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "536d39ba-09f7-5397-b275-f954b5031386" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383728} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce02ec5-6c92-51fb-a5ce-703f70a4caac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has at most one solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383729} {:answer/id #uuid "e039c579-d506-5e83-9683-664c71f89f32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has a unique solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383730} {:answer/id #uuid "cbb494d9-b4c1-54e7-8d43-f2885bb67d2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has infinitely many solutions or no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ce02ec5-6c92-51fb-a5ce-703f70a4caac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has at most one solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383729} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb808b47-23a6-56c3-875c-808edc3dd1f3" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "302bac11-c0dd-5a24-97f7-11c22c2ff5b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-339173" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-y={x}^{2}{e}^{x},\\,{y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(0) & {y}_{2}^{′}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′}(1) & {y}_{2}^{′}(1)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below **without calculating the solution** to this BVP. \n\n$\\,$ The **absolute value** of $det(A)$ is {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the corresponding homogeneous BVP {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, our inhomogeneous BVP {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the second-order inhomogeneous boundary value problem (BVP)\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $P,Q,$ and $f$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and ${\\alpha }_{i}$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real constants such that $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nThe inhomogeneous BVP has at most one solution if and only if the associated homogeneous BVP has only the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Specifically:\n\n- If the homogeneous BVP has the unique solution $y≡0,$ then the inhomogeneous problem either has no solution or a unique solution.\n\n- If the homogeneous BVP has (infinitely many) nontrivial solutions, then the inhomogeneous problem either has infinitely many solutions or no solution.\n\nTo classify the solutions of the corresponding homogeneous BVP, we define the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\,{\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{1}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}(a) & {\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{2}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}(a) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{1}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}(b) & {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{2}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}(b)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe homogeneous BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y≡0$ if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$ In other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the homogeneous BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Neumann boundary conditions. So, setting ${\\alpha }_{i}=0,{\\beta }_{i}=1,a=0,$ and $b=1,$ the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(0) & {y}_{2}^{′}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′}(1) & {y}_{2}^{′}(1)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 1.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}^{′}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}^{′}(x)=-{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{0} & -{e}^{0} \\\\ {e}^{1} & -{e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ e & -{e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(A)| & =|1\\cdot (-{e}^{-1})-(-1)\\cdot e| \\\\  & =-{e}^{-1}+e \\\\  & =e-\\frac{1}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)\\ne 0,$ the corresponding homogeneous BVP $\\text{has the unique solution }y≡0.$\n\nTherefore, since the corresponding homogeneous BVP has the unique solution $y≡0,$ our inhomogeneous BVP $\\text{has at most one solution}.$"}]}
